### [02:23:10 UTC] Step 3 — Root cause investigation for test_write_audio failure
Input: fat_llama/tests/test_feed.py (test_write_audio, lines 35-68) and fat_llama/audio_fattener/feed.py (read_audio lines 16-64, write_audio lines 66-81).
Action: Read both files. Confirmed read_audio() returns raw PCM-scale samples (magnitude in tens of thousands, native int16 range from get_array_of_samples()) while write_audio() calls sf.write(file_path, data.astype(np.float64), sample_rate, format=..., subtype='PCM_24') with no scaling/normalization step applied to `data` before writing.
Reasoning: soundfile expects float arrays passed to sf.write with an integer subtype to already be normalized to [-1, 1]; raw PCM-scale float64 values are far outside that range, so nearly every sample clips to full scale, destroying the waveform. This is a genuine source bug in write_audio(), not a test problem.
Output: Root cause identified: fat_llama/audio_fattener/feed.py write_audio() (lines 66-81) lacks amplitude scaling/normalization before writing with an integer PCM subtype.

